    "body": "I can confirm that using the MO transformed integrals does fix the problem, thank you. I think I was under the impression that `._eri` and `.get_hcore()` were the integrals in the MO basis. For the full SQD code, it does sometimes return the Hartree-Fock energy, which suggests to me that for some reason or another, the sampling step doesn't capture all of the Slater determinants needed to recover the FCI energy. This is a bit puzzling, but maybe has something to do with the ansatz used here. My guess is that it is likely not a bug. The -1.47 value is likely an artifact of whatever is causing this.\n\nIt still does seem a bit strange to me that it should matter that the 1 and 2 body integrals are in the AO basis rather than the MO basis (when using the full set of orbitals and not selecting an active space from a set of MOs obtained from a mean-field calculation or something). Mathematically, such a transformation is equivalent to a unitary transformation on the Hilbert space. The ground state energy should be invariant under such a transformation. (assuming the selected ci solver is performing the diagonalization in the full Hilbert space.) Maybe there are some details here that I am missing.",
