    "updated_at": "2025-02-05T20:25:36Z",
    "body": "> It still does seem a bit strange to me that it should matter that the 1 and 2 body integrals are in the AO basis rather than the MO basis (when using the full set of orbitals and not selecting an active space from a set of MOs obtained from a mean-field calculation or something). Mathematically, such a transformation is equivalent to a unitary transformation on the Hilbert space. The ground state energy should be invariant under such a transformation. (assuming the selected ci solver is performing the diagonalization in the full Hilbert space.) Maybe there are some details here that I am missing.\n\nhttps://pyscf.org/_modules/pyscf/fci.html#FCI\n\nI think pyscf's fci subpackage generally expects things in MO basis, but it is not very clearly documented. I'm also not too keen on the details of how things are implemented under the hood.\n\nGoing to close this issue, as translating bases resolved it. Thanks again for submitting",
